::GEO_U05_L01_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U05_L01_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CIR.8<br><br>Question:<br>A circle with radius 3 cm is compared to a circle with radius 6 cm. Which transformation proves the circles are similar? {
=A. A dilation with scale factor 2 maps the smaller circle to the larger circle.#All circles are similar because a dilation can map one radius to the other while preserving circular shape.
~B. A translation alone maps the smaller circle to the larger circle.#A translation changes location but not size, so it cannot change the radius.
~C. A reflection alone changes the radius.#A reflection preserves size and cannot produce a larger circle.
~D. No transformation can compare circles of different sizes.#A dilation is exactly the transformation that compares circles with different radii.
}

::GEO_U05_L01_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U05_L01_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CIR.8<br><br>Question:<br>Which pair of features is enough to describe the size and location of a circle? {
~A. Chord and secant#Chords and secants are related lines but do not fully define one circle.
=B. Center and radius#The center gives location and the radius determines size.
~C. Arc and tangent#An arc and tangent do not identify both the center and radius.
~D. Diameter and color#Diameter gives size, but color is not a geometric feature.
}

::GEO_U05_L01_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U05_L01_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CIR.8<br><br>Question:<br>Circle A has diameter 10 units and Circle B has diameter 30 units. What scale factor maps Circle A to Circle B? {
~A. 10#This uses the original diameter, not the ratio between diameters.
~B. 30#This uses the image diameter, not the scale factor.
=C. 3#Corresponding lengths in circles scale by the diameter ratio, so 30 divided by 10 is 3.
~D. <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub>#This is the reverse scale factor from Circle B to Circle A.
}

::GEO_U05_L01_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U05_L01_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CIR.8<br><br>Question:<br>A round table is modeled by a circle. Which statement correctly names a radius? {
~A. A segment connecting two points on the circle through the center#That describes a diameter, not a radius.
~B. A line that touches the circle at one point#That describes a tangent line.
~C. A line that intersects the circle at two points#That describes a secant line.
=D. A segment from the center to a point on the circle#A radius always connects the center of the circle to a point on the circle.
}

::GEO_U05_L01_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U05_L01_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CIR.8<br><br>Question:<br>Why does appearance alone not prove that two circular diagrams are similar? {
=A. A transformation argument using centers and radii is needed.#The proof should cite a dilation, possibly with a rigid motion, that maps one circle to the other.
~B. Any two drawings that look round are automatically proven similar.#The conclusion may be true for circles, but a proof needs mathematical reasoning.
~C. Similarity depends on the color of the diagrams.#Color is not a geometric property.
~D. Only circles with the same radius can be similar.#Circles with different radii are similar by dilation.
}

